import
	../../html

intro-matches html[]()
	- section "Matching numbers",
		- p quote
			A {match_} expression branches based on a single value.
			It can be any numeric type.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log shape-name 3
				log shape-name 4
				log shape-name 11

			shape-name string(sides nat)
				match sides
				as 3
					"triangle"
				as 4
					"square"
				else
					"\{sides\}-gon"

		- p quote
			{match_} is an expression and can be nested inside other expressions.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat = 1
				log match a
				as 1
					"OK"
				else
					"what?"

		- p "Branches of the {match_} can be squeezed onto one line using {then_}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat = 1
				log match a
				as 1 then "OK"
				else then "what?"

		- p "The argument to {match_} can introduce a local."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log match x = 3
				as 2 then "two"
				as 4 then "four"
				else then "some other number: \{x\}"

	- section "About symbols",
		- p quote
			A {symbol} is a simple value that can tested against other {symbol} values using {fn-equal}.
			{br}
			Like an {enum_} value, this kind of value is intended to be used for its identity.
			{br}
			You can't directly access the characters in a {symbol}, although
			it can be converted to or from a {string} using {std-function "keen/symbol", "to"}.
		- p quote
			Think of the {symbol} type as an infinitely large {enum_} supporting all possible values.
			{br}
			This makes it useful when you don't know all possible values up-front.
		- p quote
			If you are dealing with a limited set of values with likely repetition,
			you should usually use a {symbol}.
			{br}
			For example, the keys of a {json} object are {symbol}s.

	- section "Matching symbols and strings",
		- p "{match_} works on {symbol} and {string} values too."
		- p quote
			String values may be unquoted (as in {as_}{sp}{variable "left"} below) if they are valid Keen identifiers.
			{br}
			Otherwise they should be quoted like regular string literals.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log opposite "left"
				log opposite "right"
				log opposite "potato"

			opposite symbol(a symbol)
				match a
				as left
					"right"
				as right
					"left"
				else
					"anti-\{a\}"
		- aside-small quote
			The above would work the same with {string}, though {symbol} has better performance.

		- p "{match_} also works on {char8} or {char32}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log opposite "<"

			opposite char8(a char8)
				match a
				as l then "r"
				as r then "l"
				as "<" then ">"
				as ">" then "<"
				else then a

	- section "\"else\" branches",
		- p quote
			Unlike with an {if_} expression,
			a {match_} expression on a number or string-like type must have an {else_} branch.
			{br}
			The reason is that {match_} expressions are usually used with an enum or union,
			and in those cases no {else_} is required.
			{br}
			This will be explained in {intro-link enums} and {intro-link unions}.
